  "coverage_review_provenance_only": true,
  "conclusion": "The extra MOVE is established as a pre-IRQ instruction-transfer phase/order difference: MAME transferred the upcoming MOVE before input application, while native reaches that same prefetch tuple only after pending is established and diverts into ITLX1. Both predicates are already eligible at the observed native transfer. These two cases do not support changing only the interrupt pending gate.",
  "cause_limits": "The upstream cause of the 74-tick MOVE-boundary phase separation is not identified. Main-loop bus waits, instruction scheduling and initialization phase remain unseparated; vector-only no-extra-wait proof cannot qualify ordinary MOVE/DBRA cycles. Matching prefetch tuples are diagnostic anchors, not architectural retirement or physical correctness claims.",
  "minimum_next_diagnostic": "Use existing native instruction/bus records and MAME strip-store events to pair the last two pre-IRQ MOVE/DBRA iterations by destination address/data/order. Extract accepted request, qualified CPU latch/DTACK and micro/nano-cycle intervals; distinguish request acceptance from MAME callback. If ordinary-cycle qualification is missing, propose one narrowly bounded read-only probe for those cycles, rather than another long replay.",
  "minimum_supported_fix": "No CPU/clock/scheduler fix is justified yet. Preserve the observed reference difference and existing B16 limit while qualifying the ordinary-cycle producer.",
